#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
Hollywood Tower Hotel (The Twilight Zone Tower of Terror, Disney's Hollywood
Studios, Walt Disney World, Orlando) -- parametric model for FDM printing.

WHAT THIS IS
------------
An Autodesk Fusion script that builds a single, watertight, print-ready solid
of the Tower of Terror, auto-scaled to fit a 280 x 280 x 270 mm bed.

The building is Pueblo Deco / Spanish Colonial Revival, and almost all of its
character comes from three things a plain box stack misses entirely:

  * VERTICAL BAY ARTICULATION. The tower faces are not flat walls with punched
    windows. Full-height piers divide every face into recessed bays, and the
    piers continue past the parapet to terminate in stepped caps. This script
    derives a consistent bay rhythm for every face of every block from one
    target bay width, recesses the bays, and grows the crown's pier caps out
    of the same layout.
  * ARCHED OPENINGS. Ground-floor loggias on the wings, arched heads on the
    lowest tower windows, and a deep arcade of tall arched openings ringing
    the setback below the crown.
  * AN ASYMMETRIC PLAN. The tower sits right of centre, with a long wing and
    end pavilion running off to the left and a single shorter pavilion to the
    right -- the silhouette you see looking up Sunset Boulevard.

HOW TO RUN IT
-------------
1. Fusion "Scripts and Add-Ins" (Shift+S):
     Fusion wants a .py file inside a folder of the same name, e.g.
         .../API/Scripts/HollywoodTower/HollywoodTower.py
     so copy/rename this file accordingly before adding it. Fusion imports the
     module and calls run(context).

2. Fusion MCP / Claude extension:
     Hand the whole file to the bridge's "run python in Fusion" tool.
     AUTO_RUN below fires run(None) when the module is exec'd rather than
     imported. If your bridge uses a namespace where neither guarded name
     matches, just append an explicit `run(None)`, or set AUTO_RUN = False and
     call run(None) yourself.

Nothing here touches Fusion until run() is called, so the file is safe to
store, diff and review with no Fusion session attached.

ACCURACY -- PLEASE READ
-----------------------
Only one dimension of this building is actually published: the tower is
199 ft tall (Disney deliberately kept it under 200 ft so it would not need
FAA aircraft warning beacons). Everything else here -- footprint, bay rhythm,
setback heights, window sizes, which wing is longer -- is estimated from
photographs and marked [approx] in the dimension table. This is an honest
likeness, not a survey.

Deliberate simplifications:
  * The real wings meet the tower at a slight angle; here they are orthogonal.
  * Mission-tile roofs are smooth sloped frustums, not tiles -- tile courses
    at this scale would be ~0.3 mm, below nozzle resolution.
  * The blasted corner is a hand-tuned rectilinear pattern. Every void opens
    to the sky, so the damage adds no overhangs.
  * True-scale sign lettering would be ~1 mm tall, so the optional embossed
    text is deliberately oversized.

PRINTING NOTES
--------------
  * Orientation: as built. Z up, facade facing -Y. No rotation needed.
  * Supports: only the tile roofs need them, because they default to the real
    ~22 deg pitch (a ~68 deg overhang). Set SUPPORT_FREE_ROOFS = True to
    steepen them to 45 deg and print the whole model with no supports at all.
  * Everything else is support-free by construction. The massing only ever
    steps inward as it rises. Bay recesses are open at the top of each band or
    close with a shallow three-sided ledge. Arch heads are horizontal-axis
    arcs (short self-supporting bridges at the crown). Blast voids open to the
    sky. The one place the sign band would have bridged a recess is filled
    solid on purpose -- see SIGN_Z0/SIGN_Z1 in the tower's front face layers.
  * 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.15-0.2 mm layers. Feature sizes are audited against
    MIN_FEATURE_MM at build time and reported in the log.

Units: Fusion's API works in CENTIMETRES internally. Everything here is
authored in feet (real world) or millimetres (model) and converted at the API
boundary by _cm(). Never pass a raw model number to a Fusion call.
"""

